C) curve.\n148 | \n149 |     Extra keyword arguments will be passed to matplotlib's `plot`.\n150 | \n151 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n152 | \n153 |     Parameters\n154 |     ----------\n155 |     estimator : estimator instance\n156 |         Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n157 |         in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n158 | \n159 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n160 |         Input values.\n161 | \n162 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n163 |         Target values.\n164 | \n165 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n166 |         Sample weights.\n167 | \n168 |     drop_intermediate : boolean, default=True\n169 |         Whether to drop some suboptimal thresholds which would not appear\n170 |         on a plotted ROC curve. This is useful in order to create lighter\n171 |         ROC curves.\n172 | \n173 |     response_method : {'predict_proba', 'decision_function', 'auto'} \\\n174 |     default='auto'\n175 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n176 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n177 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n178 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n179 | \n180 |     name : str, default=None\n181 |         Name of ROC Curve for labeling. If `None`, use the name of the\n182 |         estimator.\n183 | \n184 |     ax : matplotlib axes, default=None\n185 |         Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is created.\n186 | \n187 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n188 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing the roc auc\n189 |         metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered\n190 |         as the positive class.\n191 | \n192 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n193 | \n194 |     Returns\n195 |     -------\n196 |     di