\n  68 | \n  69 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <column_transformer>`.\n  70 | \n  71 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n  72 | \n  73 |     Parameters\n  74 |     ----------\n  75 |     transformers : list of tuples\n  76 |         List of (name, transformer, columns) tuples specifying the\n  77 |         transformer objects to be applied to subsets of the data.\n  78 | \n  79 |         name : str\n  80 |             Like in Pipeline and FeatureUnion, this allows the transformer and\n  81 |             its parameters to be set using ``set_params`` and searched in grid\n  82 |             search.\n  83 |         transformer : {'drop', 'passthrough'} or estimator\n  84 |             Estimator must support :term:`fit` and :term:`transform`.\n  85 |             Special-cased strings 'drop' and 'passthrough' are accepted as\n  86 |             well, to indicate to drop the columns or to pass them through\n  87 |             untransformed, respectively.\n  88 |         columns :  str, array-like of str, int, array-like of int, \\\n  89 |                 array-like of bool, slice or callable\n  90 |             Indexes the data on its second axis. Integers are interpreted as\n  91 |             positional columns, while strings can reference DataFrame columns\n  92 |             by name.  A scalar string or int should be used where\n  93 |             ``transformer`` expects X to be a 1d array-like (vector),\n  94 |             otherwise a 2d array will be passed to the transformer.\n  95 |             A callable is passed the input data `X` and can return any of the\n  96 |             above. To select multiple columns by name or dtype, you can use\n  97 |             :obj:`make_column_selector`.\n  98 | \n  99 |     remainder : {'drop', 'passthrough'} or estimator, default='drop'\n 100 |         By default, only the specified columns in `transformers` are\n 101 |         transformed and combined in the output, and the non-specified\n 102 |         columns are dropped. (default of ``'drop'``).\n 103 |         By specifying ``remainder='passthrough'``, all remaining columns that\n 104 |         were not specified in `transformers`, but present in the data passed\n 105 |         to `fit` will be automatically passed through. This subset of columns\n 106 |         is concatenated with the output of the transformers. For dataframes,\n 107 |         extra columns not seen during `fit` will be excluded from the output\n 108 |         of `transform`.\n 109 |         By setting ``remainder`` to be an estimator, the remaining\n 110 |         non-specified columns will use the ``remainder`` estimator. The\n 111 |         estimator must support :term:`fit` and :term:`transform`.\n 112 |         Note that using this feature requires that the DataFrame columns\n 113 |         input at :term:`fit` and :term:`transform` have identical order.\n 114 | \n 115 |     sparse_threshold : float, default=0.3\n 116 |         If the output of the different transformers contains sparse matrices,\n 117 |         these will be stacked as a sparse matrix if the overall density is\n 118 |         lower than this value. Use ``sparse_threshold=0`` to always return\n 119 |         dense.  When the transformed output consists of all dense data, the\n 120 |         stacked result will be dense, and this keyword will be ignored.\n 121 | \n 122 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n 123 |         Number of jobs to run in parallel.\n 124 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n 125 |         ``-1`` means using all processors. See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>`\n 126 |         for more details.\n 127 | \n 128 |     transformer_weights : dict, default=None\n 129 |         Multiplicative weights for features per transformer. The output of the\n 130 |         transformer is multiplied by these weights. Keys are transformer names,\n 131 |         values the weights.\n 132 | \n 133 |     verbose : bool, default=False\n 134 |         If True, the time elapsed while fitting each transformer will be\n 135 |         printed as it is completed.\n 136 | \n 137 |     verbose_feature_names_out : bool, str or Callable[[str, str], str], default=True\n 138 | \n 139 |         - If True, :meth:`ColumnTransformer.get_feature_names_out` will prefix\n 140 |           all feature names with the name of the transformer that generated that\n 141 |           feature. It is equivalent to setting\n 142 |           `verbose_feature_names_out=\"{transformer_name}__{feature_name}\"`.\n 143 |         - If False, :meth:`ColumnTransformer.get_feature_names_out` will not\n 144 |           prefix any feature names and will error if feature names are not\n 145 |           unique.\n 146 |         - If ``Callable[[str, str], str]``,\n 147 |           :meth:`ColumnTransformer.get_feature_names_out` will rename all the features\n 148 |           using the name of the transformer. The first argument of the callable is the\n 149 |           transformer name and the second argument is the feature name. The returned\n 150 |           string will be the new feature name.\n 151 |         - If ``str``, it must be a string ready for formatting. The given string will\n 152 |           be formatted using two field names: ``transformer_name`` and ``feature_name``.\n 153 |           e.g. ``\"{feature_name}__{transformer_name}\"``. See :meth:`str.format` method\n 154 |           from the standard library for more info.\n 155 | \n 156 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 157 | \n 158 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.6\n 159 |             `verbose_feature_names_out` can be a callable or a string to be formatted.\n 160 | \n 161 |     force_int_remainder_cols : bool, default=True\n 162 |         Force the columns of the last entry of `transformers_`, which\n 163 |         corresponds to the \"remainder\" transformer, to always be stored as\n 164 |         indices (int) rather than column names (str). See description of the\n 165 |         `transformers_` attribute for details.\n 166 | \n 167 |         .. note::\n 168 |             If you do not access the list of columns for the remainder columns\n 169 |             in the `transformers_` fitted attribute, you do not need to set\n 170 |             this parameter.\n 171 | \n 172 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5\n 173 | \n 174 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.7\n 175 |            The default value for `force_int_remainder_cols` will change from\n 176 |            `True` to `False` in version 1.7.\n 177 | \n 178 |     Attributes\n 179 |     ----------\n 180 |     transformers_ : list\n 181 |         The collection of fitted transformers as tuples of (name,\n 182 |         fitted_transformer, column). `fitted_transformer` can be an estimator,\n 183 |         or `'drop'`; `'passthrough'` is replaced with an equivalent\n 184 |         :class:`~sklearn.preprocessing.FunctionTransformer`. In case there were\n 185 |         no columns selected, this will be the unfitted transformer. If there\n 186 |         are remaining columns, the final element is a tuple of the form:\n 187 |         ('remainder', transformer, remaining_columns) corresponding to the\n 188 |         ``remainder`` parameter. If there are remaining columns, then\n 189 |         ``len(transformers_)==len(transformers)+1``, otherwise\n 190 |         ``len(transformers_)==len(transformers)``.\n 191 | \n 192 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.5\n 193 |             If there are remaining columns and `force_int_remainder_cols` is\n 194 |             True, the remaining columns are always represented by their\n 195 |             positional indices in the input `X` (as in older versions). If\n 196 |             `force_int_remainder_cols` is False, the format attempts to match\n 197 |             that of the other transformers: if all columns were provided as\n 198 |             column names (`str`), the remaining columns are stored as column\n 199 |             names; if all columns were provided as mask arrays (`bool`), so are\n 200 |             the remaining columns; in all other cases the remaining columns are\n 201 |             stored as indices (`int`).\n 202 | \n 203 |     named_transformers_ : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n 204 |         Read-only attribute to access any transformer by given name.\n 205 |         Keys are transformer names and values are the fitted transformer\n 206 |         objects.\n 207 | \n 208 |     sparse_output_ : bool\n 209 |         Boolean flag indicating whether the output of ``transform`` is a\n 210 |         sparse matrix or a dense numpy array, which depends on the output\n 211 |         of the individual transformers and the `sparse_threshold` keyword.\n 212 | \n 213 |     output_indices_ : dict\n 214 |         A dictionary from each transformer name to a slice, where the slice\n 215 |         corresponds to indices in the transformed output. This is useful to\n 216 |         inspect which transformer is responsible for which transformed\n 217 |         feature(s).\n 218 | \n 219 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 220 | \n 221 |     n_features_in_ : int\n 222 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`. Only defined if the\n 223 |         underlying transformers expose such an attribute when fit.\n 224 | \n 225 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 226 | \n 227 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n 228 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Defined only when `X`\n 229 |         has feature names that are all strings.\n 230 | \n 231 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n 232 | \n 233 |     See Also\n 234 |     --------\n 235 |     make_column_transformer : Convenience function for\n 236 |         combining the outputs of multiple transformer objects applied to\n 237 |         column subsets of the original feature space.\n 238 |     make_column_selector : Convenience function for selecting\n 239 |         columns based on datatype or the columns name with a regex pattern.\n 240 | \n 241 |     Notes\n 242 |     -----\n 243 |     The order of the columns in the transformed feature matrix follows the\n 244 |     order of how the columns are specified in the `transformers` list.\n 245 |     Columns of the original feature matrix that are not specified are\n 246 |     dropped from the resulting transformed feature matrix, unless specified\n 247 |     in the `passthrough` keyword. Those columns specified with `passthrough`\n 248 |     are added at the right to the output of the transformers.\n 249 | \n 250 |     Examples\n 251 |     --------\n 252 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 253 |     >>> from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\n 254 |     >>> from sklearn.preprocessing import Normalizer\n 255 |     >>> ct = ColumnTransformer(\n 256 |     ...     [(\"norm1\", Normalizer(norm='l1'), [0, 1]),\n 257 |     ...      (\"norm2\", Normalizer(norm='l1'), slice(2, 4))])\n 258 |     >>> X = np.array([[0., 1., 2., 2.],\n 259 |     ...               [1., 1., 0., 1.]])\n 260 |     >>> # Normalizer scales each row of X to unit norm. A separate scaling\n 261 |     >>> # is applied for the two first and two last elements of each\n 262 |     >>> # row independently.\n 263 |     >>> ct.fit_transform(X)\n 264 |     array([[0. , 1. , 0.5, 0.5],\n 265 |            [0.5, 0.5, 0. , 1. ]])\n 266 | \n 267 |     :class:`ColumnTransformer` can be configured with a transformer that requires\n 268 |     a 1d array by setting the column to a string:\n 269 | \n 270 |     >>> from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n 271 |     >>> from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\n 272 |     >>> import pandas as pd   # doctest: +SKIP\n 273 |     >>> X = pd.DataFrame({\n 274 |     ...     \"documents\": [\"First item\", \"second one here\", \"Is this the last?\"],\n 275 |     ...     \"width\": [3, 4, 5],\n 276 |     ... })  # doctest: +SKIP\n 277 |     >>> # \"documents\" is a string which configures ColumnTransformer to\n 278 |     >>> # pass the documents column as a 1d array to the CountVectorizer\n 279 |     >>> ct = ColumnTransformer(\n 280 |     ...     [(\"text_preprocess\", CountVectorizer(), \"documents\"),\n 281 |     ...      (\"num_preprocess\", MinMaxScaler(), [\"width\"])])\n 282 |     >>> X_trans = ct.fit_transform(X)  # doctest: +SKIP\n 283 | \n 284 |     For a more detailed example of usage, see\n 285 |     :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_compose_plot_column_transformer_mixed_types.py`.\n 286 |     \"\"\"\n 287 | \n 288 |     _required_parameters = [\"transformers\"]\n 289 | \n 290 |     _parameter_constraints: dict = {\n 291 |         \"transformers\": [list, Hidden(tuple)],\n 292 |         \"remainder\": [\n 293 |             StrOptions({\"drop\", \"passthrough\"}),\n 294 |             HasMethods([\"fit\", \"transform\"]),\n 295 |             HasMethods([\"fit_transform\", \"transform\"]),\n 296 |         ],\n 297 |         \"sparse_threshold\": [Interval(Real, 0, 1, closed=\"both\")],\n 298 |         \"n_jobs\": [Integral, None],\n 299 |         \"transformer_weights\": [dict, None],\n 300 |         \"verbose\": [\"verbose\"],\n 301 |         \"verbose_feature_names_out\": [\"boolean\", str, callable],\n 302 |         \"force_int_remainder_cols\": [\"boolean\"],\n 303 |     }\n 304 | \n 305 |     def __init__(\n 306 |         self,\n 307 |         transformers,\n 308 |         *,\n 309 |         remainder=\"drop\",\n 310 |         sparse_threshold=0.3,\n 311 |         n_jobs=None,\n 312 |         transformer_weights=None,\n 313 |         verbose=False,\n 314 |         verbose_feature_names_out=True,\n 315 |         force_int_remainder_cols=True,\n 316 |     ):\n 317 |         self.transformers = transformers\n 318 |         self.remainder = remainder\n 319 |         self.sparse_threshold = sparse_threshold\n 320 |         self.n_jobs = n_jobs\n 321 |         self.transformer_weights = transformer_weights\n 322 |         self.verbose = verbose\n 323 |         self.verbose_feature_names_out = verbose_feature_names_out\n 324 |         self.force_int_remainder_cols = force_int_remainder_cols\n 325 | \n 326 |     @property\n 327 |     def _transformers(self):\n 328 |         \"\"\"\n 329 |         Internal list of transformer only containing the name and\n 330 |         transformers, dropping the columns.\n 331 | \n 332 |         DO NOT USE: This is for the implementation of get_params via\n 333 |         BaseComposition._get_params which expects lists of tuples of len 2.\n 334 | \n 335 |         To iterate through the transformers, use ``self._iter`` instead.\n 336 |         \"\"\"\n 337 |         try:\n 338 |             return [(name, trans) for name, trans, _ in self.transformers]\n 339 |         except (TypeError, ValueError):\n 340 |             return self.transformers\n 341 | \n 342 |     @_transformers.setter\n 343 |     def _transformers(self, value):\n 344 |         \"\"\"DO NOT USE: This is for the implementation of set_params via\n 345 |         BaseComposition._get_params which gives lists of tuples of len 2.\n 346 |         \"\"\"\n 347 |         try:\n 348 |             self.transformers = [\n 349 |                 (name, trans, col)\n 350 |                 for ((name, trans), (_, _, col)) in zip(value, self.transformers)\n 351 |             ]\n 352 |         except (TypeError, ValueError):\n 353 |             self.transformers = value\n 354 | \n 355 |     def set_output(self, *, transform=None):\n 356 |         \"\"\"Set the output container when `\"transform\"` and `\"fit_transform\"` are called.\n 357 | \n 358 |         Calling `set_output` will set the output of all estimators in `transformers`\n 359 |         and `transformers_`.\n 360 | \n 361 |         Parameters\n 362 |         ----------\n 363 |         transform : {\"default\", \"pandas\", \"polars\"}, default=None\n 364 |             Configure output of `transform` and `fit_transform`.\n 365 | \n 366 |             - `\"default\"`: Default output format of a transformer\n 367 |             - `\"pandas\"`: DataFrame output\n 368 |             - `\"polars\"`: Polars output\n 369 |             - `None`: Transform configuration is unchanged\n 370 | \n 371 |             .. versionadded:: 1.4\n 372 |                 `\"polars\"` option was added.\n 373 | \n 374 |         Returns\n 375 |         -------\n 376 |         self : estimator instance\n 377 |             Estimator instance.\n 378 |         \"\"\"\n 379 |         super().set_output(transform=transform)\n 380 | \n 381 |         transformers = (\n 382 |             trans\n 383 |             for _, trans, _ in chain(\n 384 |                 self.transformers, getattr(self, \"transformers_\", [])\n 385 |             )\n 386 |             if trans not in {\"passthrough\", \"drop\"}\n 387 |         )\n 388 |         for trans in transformers:\n 389 |             _safe_set_output(trans, transform=transform)\n 390 | \n 391 |         if self.remainder not in {\"passthrough\", \"drop\"}:\n 392 |             _safe_set_output(self.remainder, transform=transform)\n 393 | \n 394 |         return self\n 395 | \n 396 |     def get_params(self, deep=True):\n 397 |         \"\"\"Get parameters for this estimator.\n 398 | \n 399 |         Returns the parameters given in the constructor as well as the\n 400 |         estimators contained within the `transformers` of the\n 401 |         `ColumnTransformer`.\n 402 | \n 403 |         Parameters\n 404 |         ----------\n 405 |         deep : bool, default=True\n 406 |             If True, will return the parameters for this estimator and\n 407 |             contained subobjects that are estimators.\n 408 | \n 409 |         Returns\n 410 |         -------\n 411 |         params : dict\n 412 |             Parameter names mapped to their values.\n 413 |         \"\"\"\n 414 |         return self._get_params(\"_transformers\", deep=deep)\n 415 | \n 416 |     def set_params(self, **kwargs):\n 417 |         \"\"\"Set the parameters of this estimator.\n 418 | \n 419 |         Valid parameter keys can be listed with ``get_params()``. Note that you\n 420 |         can directly set the parameters of the estimators contained in\n 421 |         `transformers` of `ColumnTransformer`.\n 422 | \n 423 |         Parameters\n 424 |         ----------\n 425 |         **kwargs : dict\n 426 |             Estimator parameters.\n 427 | \n 428 |         Returns\n 429 |         -------\n 430 |         self : ColumnTransformer\n 431 |             This estimator.\n 432 |         \"\"\"\n 433 |         self._set_params(\"_transformers\", **kwargs)\n 434 |         return self\n 435 | \n 436 |     def _iter(self, fitted, column_as_labels, skip_drop, skip_empty_columns):\n 437 |         \"\"\"\n 438 |         Generate (name, trans, columns, weight) tuples.\n 439 | \n 440 | \n 441 |         Parameters\n 442 |         ----------\n 443 |         fitted : bool\n 444 |             If True, use the fitted transformers (``self.transformers_``) to\n 445 |             iterate through transformers, else use the transformers passed by\n 446 |             the user (``self.transformers``).\n 447 | \n 448 |         column_as_labels : bool\n 449 |             If True, columns are returned as string labels. If False, columns\n 450 |             are returned as they were given by the user. This can only be True\n 451 |             if the ``ColumnTransformer`` is already fitted.\n 452 | \n 453 |         skip_drop : bool\n 454 |             If True, 'drop' transformers are filtered out.\n 455 | \n 456 |         skip_empty_columns : bool\n 457 |             If True, transformers with empty selected columns are filtered out.\n 458 | \n 459 |         Yields\n 460 |         ------\n 461 |         A generator of tuples containing:\n 462 |             - name : the name of the transformer\n 463 |             - transformer : the transformer object\n 464 |             - columns : the columns for that transformer\n 465 |             - weight : the weight of the transformer\n 466 |         \"\"\"\n 467 |         if fitted:\n 468 |             transformers = self.transformers_\n 469 |         else:\n 470 |             # interleave the validated column specifiers\n 471 |             transformers = [\n 472 |                 (name, trans, column)\n 473 |                 for (name, trans, _), column in zip(self.transformers, self._columns)\n 474 |             ]\n 475 |             # add transformer tuple for remainder\n 476 |             if self._remainder[2]:\n 477 |                 transformers = chain(transformers, [self._remainder])\n 478 | \n 479 |         # We want the warning about the future change of the remainder\n 480 |         # columns dtype to be shown only when a user accesses them\n 481 |         # directly, not when they are used by the ColumnTransformer itself.\n 482 |         # We disable warnings here; they are enabled when setting\n 483 |         # self.transformers_.\n 484 |         transformers = _with_dtype_warning_enabled_set_to(False, transformers)\n 485 | \n 486 |         get_weight = (self.transformer_weights or {}).get\n 487 | \n 488 |         for name, trans, columns in transformers:\n 489 |             if skip_drop and trans == \"drop\":\n 490 |                 continue\n 491 |             if skip_empty_columns and _is_empty_column_selection(columns):\n 492 |                 continue\n 493 | \n 494 |             if column_as_labels:\n 495 |                 # Convert all columns to using their string labels\n 496 |                 columns_is_scalar = np.isscalar(columns)\n 497 | \n 498 |                 indices = self._transformer_to_input_indices[name]\n 499 |                 columns = self.feature_names_in_[indices]\n 500 | \n 501 |                 if columns_is_scalar:\n 502 |                     # selection is done with one dimension\n 503 |                     columns = columns[0]\n 504 | \n 505 |             yield (name, trans, columns, get_weight(name))\n 506 | \n 507 |     def _validate_transformers(self):\n 508 |         \"\"\"Validate names of transformers and the transformers themselves.\n 509 | \n 510 |         This checks whether given transformers have the required methods, i.e.\n 511 |         `fit` or `fit_transform` and `transform` implemented.\n 512 |         \"\"\"\n 513 |         if not self.transformers:\n 514 |             return\n 515 | \n 516 |         names, transformers, _ = zip(*self.transformers)\n 517 | \n 518 |         # validate names\n 519 |         self._validate_names(names)\n 520 | \n 521 |         # validate estimators\n 522 |         for t in transformers:\n 523 |             if t in (\"drop\", \"passthrough\"):\n 524 |                 continue\n 525 |             if not (hasattr(t, \"fit\") or hasattr(t, \"fit_transform\")) or not hasattr(\n 526 |                 t, \"transform\"\n 527 |             ):\n 528 |                 # Used to validate the transformers in the `transformers` list\n 529 |                 raise TypeError(\n 530 |                     \"All estimators should implement fit and \"\n 531 |                     \"transform, or can be 'drop' or 'passthrough' \"\n 532 |                     \"specifiers. '%s' (type %s) doesn't.\" % (t, type(t))\n 533 |                 )\n 534 | \n 535 |     def _validate_column_callables(self, X):\n 536 |         \"\"\"\n 537 |         Converts callable column specifications.\n 538 | \n 539 |         This stores a dictionary of the form `{step_name: column_indices}` and\n 540 |         calls the `columns` on `X` if `columns` is a callable for a given\n 541 |         transformer.\n 542 | \n 543 |         The results are then stored in `self._transformer_to_input_indices`.\n 544 |         \"\"\"\n 545 |         all_columns = []\n 546 |         transformer_to_input_indices = {}\n 547 |         for name, _, columns in self.transformers:\n 548 |             if callable(columns):\n 549 |                 columns = columns(X)\n 550 |             all_columns.append(columns)\n 551 |             transformer_to_input_indices[name] = _get_column_indices(X, columns)\n 552 | \n 553 |         self._columns = all_columns\n 554 |         self._transformer_to_input_indices = transformer_to_input_indices\n 555 | \n 556 |     def _validate_remainder(self, X):\n 557 |         \"\"\"\n 558 |         Validates ``remainder`` and defines ``_remainder`` targeting\n 559 |         the remaining columns.\n 560 |         \"\"\"\n 561 |         cols = set(chain(*self._transformer_to_input_indices.values()))\n 562 |         remaining = sorted(set(range(self.n_features_in_)) - cols)\n 563 |         self._transformer_to_input_indices[\"remainder\"] = remaining\n 564 |         remainder_cols = self._get_remainder_cols(remaining)\n 565 |         self._remainder = (\"remainder\", self.remainder, remainder_cols)\n 566 | \n 567 |     def _get_remainder_cols_dtype(self):\n 568 |         try:\n 569 |             all_dtypes = {_determine_key_type(c) for (*_, c) in self.transformers}\n 570 |             if len(all_dtypes) == 1:\n 571 |                 return next(iter(all_dtypes))\n 572 |         except ValueError:\n 573 |             # _determine_key_type raises a ValueError if some transformer\n 574 |             # columns are Callables\n 575 |             return \"int\"\n 576 |         return \"int\"\n 577 | \n 578 |     def _get_remainder_cols(self, indices):\n 579 |         dtype = self._get_remainder_cols_dtype()\n 580 |         if self.force_int_remainder_cols and dtype != \"int\":\n 581 |             return _RemainderColsList(indices, future_dtype=dtype)\n 582 |         if dtype == \"str\":\n 583 |             return list(self.feature_names_in_[indices])\n 584 |         if dtype == \"bool\":\n 585 |             return [i in indices for i in range(self.n_features_in_)]\n 586 |         return indices\n 587 | \n 588 |     @property\n 589 |     def named_transformers_(self):\n 590 |         \"\"\"Access the fitted transformer by name.\n 591 | \n 592 |         Read-only attribute to access any transformer by given name.\n 593 |         Keys are transformer names and values are the fitted transformer\n 594 |         objects.\n 595 |         \"\"\"\n 596 |         # Use Bunch object to improve autocomplete\n 597 |         return Bunch(**{name: trans for name, trans, _ in self.transformers_})\n 598 | \n 599 |     def _get_feature_name_out_for_transformer(self, name, trans, feature_names_in):\n 600 |         \"\"\"Gets feature names of transformer.\n 601 | \n 602 |         Used in conjunction with self._iter(fitted=True) in get_feature_names_out.\n 603 |         \"\"\"\n 604 |         column_indices = self._transformer_to_input_indices[name]\n 605 |         names = feature_names_in[column_indices]\n 606 |         # An actual transformer\n 607 |         if not hasattr(trans, \"get_feature_names_out\"):\n 608 |             raise AttributeError(\n 609 |                 f\"Transformer {name} (type {type(trans).__name__}) does \"\n 610 |                 \"not provide get_feature_names_out.\"\n 611 |             )\n 612 |         return trans.get_feature_names_out(names)\n 613 | \n 614 |     def get_feature_names_out(self, input_features=None):\n 615 |         \"\"\"Get output feature names for transformation.\n 616 | \n 617 |         Parameters\n 618 |         ----------\n 619 |         input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n 620 |             Input features.\n 621 | \n 622 |             - If `input_features` is `None`, then `feature_names_in_` is\n 623 |               used as feature names in. If `feature_names_in_` is not defined,\n 624 |               then the following input feature names are generated:\n 625 |               `[\"x0\", \"x1\", ..., \"x(n_features_in_ - 1)\"]`.\n 626 |             - If `input_features` is an array-like, then `input_features` must\n 627 |               match `feature_names_in_` if `feature_names_in_` is defined.\n 628 | \n 629 |         Returns\n 630 |         -------\n 631 |         feature_names_out : ndarray of str objects\n 632 |             Transformed feature names.\n 633 |         \"\"\"\n 634 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n 635 |         input_features = _check_feature_names_in(self, input_features)\n 636 | \n 637 |         # List of tuples (name, feature_names_out)\n 638 |         transformer_with_feature_names_out = []\n 639 |         for name, trans, *_ in self._iter(\n 640 |             fitted=True,\n 641 |             column_as_labels=False,\n 642 |             skip_empty_columns=True,\n 643 |             skip_drop=True,\n 644 |         ):\n 645 |             feature_names_out = self._get_feature_name_out_for_transformer(\n 646 |                 name, trans, input_features\n 647 |             )\n 648 |             if feature_names_out is None:\n 649 |                 continue\n 650 |             transformer_with_feature_names_out.append((name, feature_names_out))\n 651 | \n 652 |         if not transformer_with_feature_names_out:\n 653 |             # No feature names\n 654 |             return np.array([], dtype=object)\n 655 | \n 656 |         return self._add_prefix_for_feature_names_out(\n 657 |             transformer_with_feature_names_out\n 658 |         )\n 659 | \n 660 |     def _add_prefix_for_feature_names_out(self, transformer_with_feature_names_out):\n 661 |         \"\"\"Add prefix for feature names out that includes the transformer names.\n 662 | \n 663 |         Parameters\n 664 |         ----------\n 665 |         transformer_with_feature_names_out : list of tuples of (str, array-like of str)\n 666 |             The tuple consistent of the transformer's name and its feature names out.\n 667 | \n 668 |         Returns\n 669 |         -------\n 670 |         feature_names_out : ndarray of shape (n_features,), dtype=str\n 671 |             Transformed feature names.\n 672 |         \"\"\"\n 673 |         feature_names_out_callable = None\n 674 |         if callable(self.verbose_feature_names_out):\n 675 |             feature_names_out_callable = self.verbose_feature_names_out\n 676 |         elif isinstance(self.verbose_feature_names_out, str):\n 677 |             feature_names_out_callable = partial(\n 678 |                 _feature_names_out_with_str_format,\n 679 |                 str_format=self.verbose_feature_names_out,\n 680 |             )\n 681 |         elif self.verbose_feature_names_out is True:\n 682 |             feature_names_out_callable = partial(\n 683 |                 _feature_names_out_with_str_format,\n 684 |                 str_format=\"{transformer_name}__{feature_name}\",\n 685 |             )\n 686 | \n 687 |         if feature_names_out_callable is not None:\n 688 |             # Prefix the feature names out with the transformers name\n 689 |             names = list(\n 690 |                 chain.from_iterable(\n 691 |                     (feature_names_out_callable(name, i) for i in feature_names_out)\n 692 |                     for name, feature_names_out in transformer_with_feature_names_out\n 693 |                 )\n 694 |             )\n 695 |             return np.asarray(names, dtype=object)\n 696 | \n 697 |         # verbose_feature_names_out is False\n 698 |         # Check that names are all unique without a prefix\n 699 |         feature_names_count = Counter(\n 700 |             chain.from_iterable(s for _, s in transformer_with_feature_names_out)\n 701 |         )\n 702 |         top_6_overlap = [\n 703 |             name for name, count in feature_names_count.most_common(6) if count > 1\n 704 |         ]\n 705 |         top_6_overlap.sort()\n 706 |         if top_6_overlap:\n 707 |             if len(top_6_overlap) == 6:\n 708 |                 # There are more than 5 overlapping names, we only show the 5\n 709 |                 # of the feature names\n 710 |                 names_repr = str(top_6_overlap[:5])[:-1] + \", ...]\"\n 711 |             else:\n 712 |                 names_repr = str(top_6_overlap)\n 713 |             raise ValueError(\n 714 |                 f\"Output feature names: {names_repr} are not unique. Please set \"\n 715 |                 \"verbose_feature_names_out=True to add prefixes to feature names\"\n 716 |             )\n 717 | \n 718 |         return np.concatenate(\n 719 |             [name for _, name in transformer_with_feature_names_out],\n 720 |         )\n 721 | \n 722 |     def _update_fitted_transformers(self, transformers):\n 723 |         \"\"\"Set self.transformers_ from given transformers.\n 724 | \n 725 |         Parameters\n 726 |         ----------\n 727 |         transformers : list of estimators\n 728 |             The fitted estimators as the output of\n 729 |             `self._call_func_on_transformers(func=_fit_transform_one, ...)`.\n 730 |             That function doesn't include 'drop' or transformers for which no\n 731 |             column is selected. 'drop' is kept as is, and for the no-column\n 732 |             transformers the unfitted transformer is put in\n 733 |             `self.transformers_`.\n 734 |         \"\"\"\n 735 |         # transformers are fitted; excludes 'drop' cases\n 736 |         fitted_transformers = iter(transformers)\n 737 |         transformers_ = []\n 738 | \n 739 |         for name, old, column, _ in self._iter(\n 740 |             fitted=False,\n 741 |             column_as_labels=False,\n 742 |             skip_drop=False,\n 743 |             skip_empty_columns=False,\n 744 |         ):\n 745 |             if old == \"drop\":\n 746 |                 trans = \"drop\"\n 747 |             elif _is_empty_column_selection(column):\n 748 |                 trans = old\n 749 |             else:\n 750 |                 trans = next(fitted_transformers)\n 751 |             transformers_.append((name, trans, column))\n 752 | \n 753 |         # sanity check that transformers is exhausted\n 754 |         assert not list(fitted_transformers)\n 755 |         self.transformers_ = _with_dtype_warning_enabled_set_to(True, transformers_)\n 756 | \n 757 |     def _validate_output(self, result):\n 758 |         \"\"\"\n 759 |         Ensure that the output of each transformer is 2D. Otherwise\n 760 |         hstack can raise an error or produce incorrect results.\n 761 |         \"\"\"\n 762 |         names = [\n 763 |             name\n 764 |             for name, _, _, _ in self._iter(\n 765 |                 fitted=True,\n 766 |                 column_as_labels=False,\n 767 |                 skip_drop=True,\n 768 |                 skip_empty_columns=True,\n 769 |             )\n 770 |         ]\n 771 |         for Xs, name in zip(result, names):\n 772 |             if not getattr(Xs, \"ndim\", 0) == 2 and not hasattr(Xs, \"__dataframe__\"):\n 773 |                 raise ValueError(\n 774 |                     \"The output of the '{0}' transformer should be 2D (numpy array, \"\n 775 |                     \"scipy sparse array, dataframe).\".format(name)\n 776 |                 )\n 777 |         if _get_output_config(\"transform\", self)[\"dense\"] == \"pandas\":\n 778 |             return\n 779 |         try:\n 780 |             import pandas as pd\n 781 |         except ImportError:\n 782 |             return\n 783 |         for Xs, name in zip(result, names):\n 784 |             if not _is_pandas_df(Xs):\n 785 |                 continue\n 786 |             for col_name, dtype in Xs.dtypes.to_dict().items():\n 787 |                 if getattr(dtype, \"na_value\", None) is not pd.NA:\n 788 |                     continue\n 789 |                 if pd.NA not in Xs[col_name].values:\n 790 |                     continue\n 791 |                 class_name = self.__class__.__name__\n 792 |                 # TODO(1.6): replace warning with ValueError\n 793 |                 warnings.warn(\n 794 |                     (\n 795 |                         f\"The output of the '{name}' transformer for column\"\n 796 |                         f\" '{col_name}' has dtype {dtype} and uses pandas.NA to\"\n 797 |                         \" represent null values. Storing this output in a numpy array\"\n 798 |                         \" can cause errors in downstream scikit-learn estimators, and\"\n 799 |                         \" inefficiencies. Starting with scikit-learn version 1.6, this\"\n 800 |                         \" will raise a ValueError. To avoid this problem you can (i)\"\n 801 |                         \" store the output in a pandas DataFrame by using\"\n 802 |                         f\" {class_name}.set_output(transform='pandas') or (ii) modify\"\n 803 |                         f\" the input data or the '{name}' transformer to avoid the\"\n 804 |                         \" presence of pandas.NA (for example by using\"\n 805 |                         \" pandas.DataFrame.astype).\"\n 806 |                     ),\n 807 |                     FutureWarning,\n 808 |                 )\n 809 | \n 810 |     def _record_output_indices(self, Xs):\n 811 |         \"\"\"\n 812 |         Record which transformer produced which column.\n 813 |         \"\"\"\n 814 |         idx = 0\n 815 |         self.output_indices_ = {}\n 816 | \n 817 |         for transformer_idx, (name, _, _, _) in enumerate(\n 818 |             self._iter(\n 819 |                 fitted=True,\n 820 |                 column_as_labels=False,\n 821 |                 skip_drop=True,\n 822 |                 skip_empty_columns=True,\n 823 |             )\n 824 |         ):\n 825 |             n_columns = Xs[transformer_idx].shape[1]\n 826 |             self.output_indices_[name] = slice(idx, idx + n_columns)\n 827 |             idx += n_columns\n 828 | \n 829 |         # `_iter` only generates transformers that have a non empty\n 830 |         # selection. Here we set empty slices for transformers that\n 831 |         # generate no output, which are safe for indexing\n 832 |         all_names = [t[0] for t in self.transformers] + [\"remainder\"]\n 833 |         for name in all_names:\n 834 |             if name not in self.output_indices_:\n 835 |                 self.output_indices_[name] = slice(0, 0)\n 836 | \n 837 |     def _log_message(self, name, idx, total):\n 838 |         if not self.verbose:\n 839 |             return None\n 840 |         return \"(%d of %d) Processing %s\" % (idx, total, name)\n 841 | \n 842 |     def _call_func_on_transformers(self, X, y, func, column_as_labels, routed_params):\n 843 |         \"\"\"\n 844 |         Private function to fit and/or transform on demand.\n 845 | \n 846 |         Parameters\n 847 |         ----------\n 848 |         X : {array-like, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 849 |             The data to be used in fit and/or transform.\n 850 | \n 851 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n 852 |             Targets.\n 853 | \n 854 |         func : callable\n 855 |             Function to call, which can be _fit_transform_one or\n 856 |             _transform_one.\n 857 | \n 858 |         column_as_labels : bool\n 859 |             Used to iterate through transformers. If True, columns are returned\n 860 |             as strings. If False, columns are returned as they were given by\n 861 |             the user. Can be True only if the ``ColumnTransformer`` is already\n 862 |             fitted.\n 863 | \n 864 |         routed_params : dict\n 865 |             The routed parameters as the output from ``process_routing``.\n 866 | \n 867 |         Returns\n 868 |         -------\n 869 |         Return value (transformers and/or transformed X data) depends\n 870 |         on the passed function.\n 871 |         \"\"\"\n 872 |         if func is _fit_transform_one:\n 873 |             fitted = False\n 874 |         else:  # func is _transform_one\n 875 |             fitted = True\n 876 | \n 877 |         transformers = list(\n 878 |             self._iter(\n 879 |                 fitted=fitted,\n 880 |                 column_as_labels=column_as_labels,\n 881 |                 skip_drop=True,\n 882 |                 skip_empty_columns=True,\n 883 |             )\n 884 |         )\n 885 |         try:\n 886 |             jobs = []\n 887 |             for idx, (name, trans, columns, weight) in enumerate(transformers, start=1):\n 888 |                 if func is _fit_transform_one:\n 889 |                     if trans == \"passthrough\":\n 890 |                         output_config = _get_output_config(\"transform\", self)\n 891 |                         trans = FunctionTransformer(\n 892 |                             accept_sparse=True,\n 893 |                             check_inverse=False,\n 894 |                             feature_names_out=\"one-to-one\",\n 895 |                         ).set_output(transform=output_config[\"dense\"])\n 896 | \n 897 |                     extra_args = dict(\n 898 |                         message_clsname=\"ColumnTransformer\",\n 899 |                         message=self._log_message(name, idx, len(transformers)),\n 900 |                     )\n 901 |                 else:  # func is _transform_one\n 902 |                     extra_args = {}\n 903 |                 jobs.append(\n 904 |                     delayed(func)(\n 905 |                         transformer=clone(trans) if not fitted else trans,\n 906 |                         X=_safe_indexing(X, columns, axis=1),\n 907 |                         y=y,\n 908 |                         weight=weight,\n 909 |                         **extra_args,\n 910 |                         params=routed_params[name],\n 911 |                     )\n 912 |                 )\n 913 | \n 914 |             return Parallel(n_jobs=self.n_jobs)(jobs)\n 915 | \n 916 |         except ValueError as e:\n 917 |             if \"Expected 2D array, got 1D array instead\" in str(e):\n 918 |                 raise ValueError(_ERR_MSG_1DCOLUMN) from e\n 919 |             else:\n 920 |                 raise\n 921 | \n 922 |     def fit(self, X, y=None, **params):\n 923 |         \"\"\"Fit all transformers using X.\n 924 | \n 925 |         Parameters\n 926 |         ----------\n 927 |         X : {array-like, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 928 |             Input data, of which specified subsets are used to fit the\n 929 |             transformers.\n 930 | \n 931 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,...), default=None\n 932 |             Targets for supervised learning.\n 933 | \n 934 |         **params : dict, default=None\n 935 |             Parameters to be passed to the underlying transformers' ``fit`` and\n 936 |             ``transform`` methods.\n 937 | \n 938 |             You can only pass this if metadata routing is enabled, which you\n 939 |             can enable using ``sklearn.set_config(enable_metadata_routing=True)``.\n 940 | \n 941 |             .. versionadded:: 1.4\n 942 | \n 943 |         Returns\n 944 |         -------\n 945 |         self : ColumnTransformer\n 946 |             This estimator.\n 947 |         \"\"\"\n 948 |         _raise_for_params(params, self, \"fit\")\n 949 |         # we use fit_transform to make sure to set sparse_output_ (for which we\n 950 |         # need the transformed data) to have consistent output type in predict\n 951 |         self.fit_transform(X, y=y, **params)\n 952 |         return self\n 953 | \n 954 |     @_fit_context(\n 955 |         # estimators in ColumnTransformer.transformers are not validated yet\n 956 |         prefer_skip_nested_validation=False\n 957 |     )\n 958 |     def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, **params):\n 959 |         \"\"\"Fit all transformers, transform the data and concatenate results.\n 960 | \n 961 |         Parameters\n 962 |         ----------\n 963 |         X : {array-like, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 964 |             Input data, of which specified subsets are used to fit the\n 965 |             transformers.\n 966 | \n 967 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n 968 |             Targets for supervised learning.\n 969 | \n 970 |         **params : dict, default=None\n 971 |             Parameters to be passed to the underlying transformers' ``fit`` and\n 972 |             ``transform`` methods.\n 973 | \n 974 |             You can only pass this if metadata routing is enabled, which you\n 975 |             can enable using ``sklearn.set_config(enable_metadata_routing=True)``.\n 976 | \n 977 |             .. versionadded:: 1.4\n 978 | \n 979 |         Returns\n 980 |         -------\n 981 |         X_t : {array-like, sparse matrix} of \\\n 982 |                 shape (n_samples, sum_n_components)\n 983 |             Horizontally stacked results of transformers. sum_n_components is the\n 984 |             sum of n_components (output dimension) over transformers. If\n 985 |             any result is a sparse matrix, everything will be converted to\n 986 |             sparse matrices.\n 987 |         \"\"\"\n 988 |         _raise_for_params(params, self, \"fit_transform\")\n 989 |         self._check_feature_names(X, reset=True)\n 990 | \n 991 |         X = _check_X(X)\n 992 |         # set n_features_in_ attribute\n 993 |         self._check_n_features(X, reset=True)\n 994 |         self._validate_transformers()\n 995 |         n_samples = _num_samples(X)\n 996 | \n 997 |         self._validate_column_callables(X)\n 998 |         self._validate_remainder(X)\n 999 | \n1000 |         if _routing_enabled():\n1001 |             routed_params = process_routing(self, \"fit_transform\", **params)\n1002 |         else:\n1003 |             routed_params = self._get_empty_routing()\n1004 | \n1005 |         result = self._call_func_on_transformers(\n1006 |             X,\n1007 |             y,\n1008 |             _fit_transform_one,\n1009 |             column_as_labels=False,\n1010 |             routed_params=routed_params,\n1011 |         )\n1012 | \n1013 |         if not result:\n1014 |             self._update_fitted_transformers([])\n1015 |             # All transformers are None\n1016 |             return np.zeros((n_samples, 0))\n1017 | \n1018 |         Xs, transformers = zip(*result)\n1019 | \n1020 |         # determine if concatenated output will be sparse or not\n1021 |         if any(sparse.issparse(X) for X in Xs):\n1022 |             nnz = sum(X.nnz if sparse.issparse(X) else X.size for X in Xs)\n1023 |             total = sum(\n1024 |                 X.shape[0] * X.shape[1] if sparse.issparse(X) else X.size for X in Xs\n1025 |             )\n1026 |             density = nnz / total\n1027 |             self.sparse_output_ = density < self.sparse_threshold\n1028 |         else:\n1029 |             self.sparse_output_ = False\n1030 | \n1031 |         self._update_fitted_transformers(transformers)\n1032 |         self._validate_output(Xs)\n1033 |         self._record_output_indices(Xs)\n1034 | \n1035 |         return self._hstack(list(Xs), n_samples=n_samples)\n1036 | \n1037 |     def transform(self, X, **params):\n1038 |         \"\"\"Transform X separately by each transformer, concatenate results.\n1039 | \n1040 |         Parameters\n1041 |         ----------\n1042 |         X : {array-like, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1043 |             The data to be transformed by subset.\n1044 | \n1045 |         **params : dict, default=None\n1046 |             Parameters to be passed to the underlying transformers' ``transform``\n1047 |             method.\n1048 | \n1049 |             You can only pass this if metadata routing is enabled, which you\n1050 |             can enable using ``sklearn.set_config(enable_metadata_routing=True)``.\n1051 | \n1052 |             .. versionadded:: 1.4\n1053 | \n1054 |         Returns\n1055 |         -------\n1056 |         X_t : {array-like, sparse matrix} of \\\n1057 |                 shape (n_samples, sum_n_components)\n1058 |             Horizontally stacked results of transformers. sum_n_components is the\n1059 |             sum of n_components (output dimension) over transformers. If\n1060 |             any result is a sparse matrix, everything 