aise AttributeError\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/errors/__init__.py:AbstractMethodError`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n300 | class AbstractMethodError(NotImplementedError):\n301 |     \"\"\"\n302 |     Raise this error instead of NotImplementedError for abstract methods.\n303 | \n304 |     The `AbstractMethodError` is designed for use in classes that follow an abstract\n305 |     base class pattern. By raising this error in the method, it ensures that a subclass\n306 |     must implement the method to provide specific functionality. This is useful in a\n307 |     framework or library where certain methods must be implemented by the user to\n308 |     ensure correct behavior.\n309 | \n310 |     Parameters\n311 |     ----------\n312 |     class_instance : object\n313 |         The instance of the class where the abstract method is being called.\n314 |     methodtype : str, default \"method\"\n315 |         A string indicating the type of method that is abstract.\n316 |         Must be one of {\"method\", \"classmethod\", \"staticmethod\", \"property\"}.\n317 | \n318 |     See Also\n319 |     --------\n320 |     api.extensions.ExtensionArray\n321 |         An example of a pandas extension mechanism that requires implementing\n322 |         specific abstract methods.\n323 |     NotImplementedError\n324 |         A built-in exception that can also be used for abstract methods but lacks\n325 |         the specificity of `AbstractMethodError` in indicating the need for subclass\n326 |         implementation.\n327 | \n328 |     Examples\n329 |     --------\n330 |     >>> class Foo:\n331 |     ...     @classmethod\n332 |     ...     def classmethod(cls):\n333 |     ...         raise pd.errors.AbstractMethodError(cls, methodtype=\"classmethod\")\n334 |     ...\n335 |     ...     def method(self):\n336 |     ...         raise pd.errors.AbstractMethodError(self)\n337 |     >>> test = Foo.classmethod()\n338 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n339 |     AbstractMethodError: This classmethod must be defined in the concrete class Foo\n340 | \n341 |     >>> test2 = Foo().method()\n342 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n343 |     AbstractMethodError: This classmethod must be defined in the concrete class Foo\n344 |     \"\"\"\n345 | \n346 |     def __init__(self, class_instance, methodtype: str = \"method\") -> None:\n347 |         types = {\"method\", \"classmethod\", \"staticmethod\", \"property\"}\n348 |         if methodtype not in types:\n349 |             raise ValueError(\n350 |                 f\"methodtype must be one of {methodtype}, got {types} instead.\"\n351 |             )\n352 |         self.methodtype = methodtype\n353 |         self.class_instance = class_instance\n354 | \n355 |     def __str__(self) -> str:\n356 |         if self.methodtype == \"classmethod\":\n357 |             name = self.class_instance.__name__\n358 |         else:\n359 |             name = type(self.class_instance).__name__\n360 |         return f\"This {self.methodtype} must be defined in the concrete class {name}\"\n```\n\nFound class `pandas/errors/__init__.py:AttributeConflictWarning`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n648 | class AttributeConflictWarning(Warning):\n649 |     \"\"\"\n650 |     Warning raised when index attributes conflict when using HDFStore.\n651 | \n652 |     Occurs when attempting to append an index with a different\n653 |     name than the existing index on an HDFStore or attempting to append an index with a\n654 |     different frequency than the existing index on an HDFStore.\n655 | \n656 |     Examples\n657 |     --------\n658 |     >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\"], name=\"name1\")\n659 |     >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], index=idx1)\n660 |     >>> df1.to_hdf(\"file\", \"data\", \"w\", append=True)  # doctest: +SKIP\n661 |     >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"c\", \"d\"], name=\"name2\")\n662 |     >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame([[5, 6], [7, 8]],